    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d827">

                <pb facs="1606" n="1566" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v09p1/pg1566"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">684A.85/5–2454: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 827<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Jordan (Mallory) to the Department of State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d827-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Amman</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-05-24T14:00:00+02:00">May 24, 1954—2 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>480. Re Deptel 443.<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d827-f2"/> British Ambassador Duke made <hi rendition="#italic">démarche</hi> to Foreign
                    Minister Saturday, May 22 and subsequently to Prime Minister. I informed Foreign
                    Minister we fully associated ourselves with British <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >démarche</hi> and deeply felt necessity of achieving relaxations of
                    tensions on border. Foreign Minister surprisingly informed me he submitting
                    question to Jordan Cabinet today. Minister from standpoint of national policy
                    stated his desire for quiet on the border and necessity of breathing space for
                    new government which inherited difficult public opinion (see also Embtel 463).<anchor n="3" xml:id="d827-f3"/>
                    Seeing Foreign Minister again Wednesday.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d827-f4"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Mallory</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d827-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d827-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to Tel Aviv and London.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d827-f2" n="2" xml:id="d827-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed, but see <ref target="#d826-f3">footnote 3, <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >supra</hi></ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d827-f3" n="3" xml:id="d827-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d827-f4" n="4" xml:id="d827-f4-content">
                <p>Ambassador Mallory forwarded, under cover
                    of despatch 408 from Amman, May 27, a memorandum of his conversation with
                    Ambassador Duke concerning the letter’s
                    approach to the Jordanian Government. Mallory also forwarded memoranda of his own conversations with
                    the Jordanian Foreign Minister and Prime Minister (on May 24 and 27
                    respectively) in which he formally stated that the United States associated
                    itself with the British <hi rendition="#italic">démarche</hi>. (684A.85/5–2654)
                    The 11 points of the British <hi rendition="#italic">démarche</hi> to Jordan
                    were not specifically described in these memoranda of conversation or in other
                    telegrams or despatches to or from Amman, but were similar to points 2 and 4–13
                    in part III B of the Department’s CA–6175, <ref
                        target="#d811">Document 811</ref>. With respect to the subsequent
                    transmittal to the Israeli Government of the same 11 suggested measures, see the
                        <hi rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> of June 19, <ref target="#d836"
                        >Document 836</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
